Based on the following character profiles and world-building settings, generate **knowledge boundaries** for each character at the start of the story.

## What Are Knowledge Boundaries

This work employs limited-perspective narration — each character can only know information obtainable through **their own perception, being told by others, or reasonable inference**.
Knowledge boundaries describe the information state a character already holds **before the first page of the story turns**, which determines:

- What facts the character can naturally mention, reference, or act upon in the narrative
- What information the character **should not know** (if it appears in the text, it constitutes information leakage)
- Under what conditions the character's **misconceptions** will be revealed (creating tension for future plot)
- The character's **sensory access limitations** — information physically impossible to obtain (e.g., conversations through walls, actions in sealed rooms)

